{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789397647,"nonce":"9kTOe1YMAXLlQZe+5TgcQPqljZw=","title":"Python: a dict made in a loop is never released","message":"pressure/dicts.py, `d = {\"a\": i, \"b\": i + 1}; d[\"c\"] = i + 2; total +=\nd[\"c\"] - d[\"a\"]` per step, grows by about 580 bytes a step (32 MB at\n2,000 steps, 141 MB at 200,000).\n\nA dict is a `List\u003cAny\u003e` of key, value pairs boxed under the dict tag.\nThe literal allocates the list header and buffer, six boxes for the\nkeys and values (the literal keys are boxed anew each step), and the\ndict box; `d[\"c\"] = ...` grows the buffer. The dict box escapes into\n`zb_dict_set` and `zb_dict_get` (Shared parameters), so nothing is\nreleased, and even with those inlined the pair boxes are list elements\nthe list does not own.\n\nWhat would fix it: Borrowed parameters on the dict library functions\nthat only read (e132f21 covers the analysis), a dict release that\nfrees the header, the buffer and the box, and for the pair boxes either\nownership by the container or a collector (b94b4cb).","files":null}]}